---
/**
 * Hero — headline, subline, vendor line, typed-terminal transcript, the
 * ledger proof strip, install one-liner and the two calls to action.
 *
 * Copy is rendered verbatim from docs/copy.md §Hero, §Hero-terminal and
 * §Proof (ADR-0003: the copy deck is the single source of truth). The
 * transcript is
 * genuine `metis next -o json` output from this repository's own ledger and
 * is reproduced byte-for-byte — it is evidence, not decoration, so it must
 * not be reflowed, re-indented or "improved".
 *
 * The CTA labels carry no arrow: in docs/copy.md the "→" separates the label
 * from its URL (compare §NotFound, where "← Back to metis" is written inline
 * because there the arrow IS part of the label).
 *
 * Animation (ADR-0002): pure CSS, zero JS. The command line types itself with
 * steps(), the JSON output then prints a line at a time. Each individual
 * animation stays inside ADR-0002's 200-700ms envelope; the composed sequence
 * runs ~2.6s, inside the 2-4s the brief asks for. Nothing loops.
 *
 * Reduced motion: every animation property lives inside a
 * `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference` block, so the base rules ARE the
 * final frame. This is deliberate — global.css neutralises animation-duration
 * under `reduce` but not animation-delay, so an opacity:0 base would leave the
 * transcript blank for seconds for exactly the users who asked for less motion.
 *
 * Note the `{"{"}` / `{"}"}` escapes on the two brace-only transcript lines:
 * a bare brace opens an Astro expression. They are written as expressions
 * returning the literal character rather than as HTML entities, so the built
 * output contains a real "{" and the byte-for-byte grep holds.
 *
 * --- Proof strip (phase-2-ws-2.4, ADR-0008) ------------------------------
 *
 * Three ledger-measured numbers plus one labeled estimate. Under ADR-0008 the
 * numbers are static strings carrying an as-of date, not fetched values
 * (OVERVIEW §2.2 forbids client-side data fetching), and each one has a
 * verification note in docs/copy.md §Proof that the reviewer recomputes. Two
 * things here are load-bearing and must survive any edit:
 *
 *   - Stat 4's "est." prefix and "at best observed" qualifier. It is the one
 *     figure on the site that is NOT ledger-recomputable, and ADR-0008 admits
 *     it only because it is visibly labeled. Dropping either word is a P2.
 *   - The caption's "as of 2026-07-26". The date is what carries the claim
 *     when the ledger moves on; removing it is a finding. It has already
 *     moved: at the time of writing `.metis/slices-done.yaml` holds 10
 *     entries, not 9 — see this slice's brief for why that is a deferral to a
 *     refresh chore rather than an edit here (docs/copy.md is read-only to
 *     this slice, and f-010 established that touching it out of scope is a
 *     P2). The claim itself still holds at 10/10: every entry's reviewer slug
 *     differs from its coder.
 *
 * Placement: immediately after the terminal figure, because §Proof says
 * "beneath the terminal caption" and the caption is the figure's last child.
 * §Hero separately calls the install one-liner "beneath the terminal", so the
 * two instructions read as a sequence — terminal, then strip, then install —
 * and the strip lands between them rather than after the CTAs.
 *
 * `−55%` opens with U+2212 MINUS SIGN, not a hyphen-minus, matching the deck
 * byte-for-byte.
 *
 * The vendor line's `AGENTS.md` is backticked in the deck and renders as plain
 * text here, the same call Protocol.astro:27-30 made for `metis next` and
 * Install.astro:19-21 for the `go install` line: a <code> element inside the
 * sentence would split it into three text nodes and break the literal grep,
 * and that is not a trade worth making for typography alone. The stat values
 * are backticked in the deck for the same reason — to pin the exact string —
 * and are likewise not <code>; the deck and the plan both call for them in the
 * display face, which is what .stat dt gives them.
 *
 * The caption is the one place the rule bends, and only where it must: a
 * markdown link is functional, not typographic, so "audit the ledger" is a
 * real <a>. It splits there and nowhere else, with the trailing "." outside
 * the anchor because that is where the deck puts it — Install.astro:12-17
 * settled this shape.
 *
 * The strip carries no `data-reveal` and no `.reveal`: the hero is above the
 * fold and renders immediately (§2.1), and the strip is part of the hero.
 * ADR-0005's observer never sees this subtree, and no script is added.
 *
 * Presentation deviates from plan §2.4 on two points, on the product owner's
 * direction after reviewing the first cut (recorded in this slice's brief
 * under "Scope amendment"). §2.4 asks for a 2×2 grid with the values "styled
 * display"; this renders them mono, in aegean teal, as a hairline-divided row
 * that goes four-across at 1024px. The reasoning:
 *
 *   - Mono is the site's idiom for machine output — the same face as the
 *     transcript immediately above. A ledger reading set in the display face
 *     reads as a marketing stat; set in mono it reads as something the tool
 *     printed, which is the claim the section is making.
 *   - Teal is tokens.css's success colour and these are the successes. Gold
 *     was rejected: the hero already spends it on the caret and the primary
 *     CTA, and OVERVIEW §3.2 asks for it sparingly.
 *   - The estimate is muted rather than teal, so measured-vs-estimated is
 *     legible before a word is read.
 *   - Four across removes the dead space 2×2 left in the wide tracks. The
 *     2×2 the plan asked for survives as the 640-1023px band.
 *
 * Lightning CSS rewrites the `min-width` queries below to `width>=` in the
 * built stylesheet. That is the same transform f-004 recorded as advisory for
 * Personas.astro, not a new behaviour.
 */
---

<section id="hero" class="section hero">
  <h1 class="headline">Your agents are brilliant. Their process isn't.</h1>

  <!-- Kept on one source line: HTML would collapse a wrapped version to the
       same rendered text, but this way the built output greps literally
       against docs/copy.md §Hero. -->
  <p class="subline">metis is the meta-harness for AI coding agents — deterministic dispatch, scope as a contract, and cross-vendor review, enforced by exit codes instead of hope.</p>

  <p class="vendor">Agent-agnostic by design — Claude Code, opencode, Codex, or any surface that reads AGENTS.md. One protocol, zero lock-in.</p>

  <figure class="terminal">
    <pre class="transcript"><code><span class="line cmd">$ metis next -o json</span>
<span class="line out">{"{"}</span>
<span class="line out">  "active": true,</span>
<span class="line out">  "id": "phase-1-ws-1.1",</span>
<span class="line out">  "title": "Hero with typed-terminal animation",</span>
<span class="line out">  "risk": "medium",</span>
<span class="line out">  "role": "Coder",</span>
<span class="line out">  "agent_slug": "claude-code/opus",</span>
<span class="line out">  ...</span>
<span class="line out">{"}"}</span>
<span class="line out prompt">$ █</span></code></pre>
    <figcaption>This very page is a slice in metis's own ledger.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="proof">
    <p class="kicker" id="proof-kicker">Dogfood numbers — this site, built under its own protocol</p>

    <dl class="stats" aria-labelledby="proof-kicker">
      <div class="stat">
        <dt>9/9</dt>
        <dd>slices independently reviewed — never by their author</dd>
      </div>

      <div class="stat">
        <dt>10</dt>
        <dd>findings caught in review, all after the author had called the work done</dd>
      </div>

      <div class="stat">
        <dt>0</dt>
        <dd>out-of-scope files shipped — file scope is machine-audited on every slice</dd>
      </div>

      <div class="stat stat--est">
        <dt>−55%</dt>
        <dd>est. token spend at best observed — one bounded slice at a time replaces session-long re-exploration</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="proof-caption">Three numbers measured from this repository's own ledger as of 2026-07-26; the fourth is a labeled estimate — <a
        href="https://github.com/techspeque/metiswww"
        rel="noopener">audit the ledger</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <pre
    class="install"><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/techspeque/metis/main/scripts/install.sh | bash</code></pre>

  <p class="ctas">
    <a class="cta cta--primary" href="https://github.com/techspeque/metis" rel="noopener">
      View on GitHub
    </a>
    <a
      class="cta cta--secondary"
      href="https://github.com/techspeque/metis/tree/main/docs"
      rel="noopener"
    >
      Read the docs
    </a>
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .section {
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* --- Copy ------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .headline {
    /* Scales between two type-scale tokens — no raw font sizes. */
    font-size: clamp(var(--text-2xl), 6vw, var(--text-4xl));
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .subline {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* Muted on background: 8.39:1 dark / 5.66:1 light (tokens.css ratio table),
     both AA. Smaller than the subline so it reads as a qualifier on the claim
     above it rather than a second claim. */
  .vendor {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* --- Terminal --------------------------------------------------------- */
  .terminal {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }

  .transcript {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* Each line is its own box so the command can be width-clipped and the
     output lines can be revealed independently. inline-block keeps the
     literal newlines between them working as line breaks inside <pre>. */
  .line {
    display: inline-block;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  /* The trailing prompt line, owl-gold: this colours the "█" caret that is
     part of the transcript itself. Contrast (tokens.css table): #D4A24E on
     surface #171B22 = 7.46:1 dark; #8A5A12 on #FFFFFF ≥ 5.53:1 light. */
  .prompt {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .terminal figcaption {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* --- Proof strip ------------------------------------------------------ */
  .proof {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* Muted on background, 8.39:1 dark / 5.66:1 light — AA. */
  .kicker {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* The rules above and below turn the four numbers into one horizontal band
     — a ledger row rather than four loose figures. They are the strip's only
     ornament: --color-border is the same decorative 1.40:1 hairline the
     terminal and the persona columns use, carrying no information (the <dl>
     structure does that), so it has no contrast obligation.

     Mobile-first: one column, no vertical dividers, no media query. */
  .stats {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    padding-block: var(--space-4);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* 640px and 1024px are raw lengths on purpose, the same trade
     Personas.astro:51-53 names: tokens.css owns colour, type and spacing and
     deliberately defines no breakpoint scale, so a layout threshold has to be
     written literally.

     Two rows of two, then one row of four. The column gap goes to zero here
     because the divider rule now does the separating and the cell padding
     supplies the air — a gap on top of both would read as a broken table. */
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .stats {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
      column-gap: 0;
      row-gap: var(--space-5);
    }

    .stat {
      padding-inline: var(--space-4);
      border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    }

    /* First cell of each row: no divider, and flush with the left edge so the
       numbers align with the headline above them. */
    .stat:nth-child(odd) {
      padding-inline-start: 0;
      border-inline-start: 0;
    }
  }

  /* One row of four. Every cell but the first regains its divider, which means
     undoing the 2-column rule for the third cell — it is no longer the start
     of a row. */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .stats {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    }

    .stat:nth-child(odd) {
      padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
      border-inline-start: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    }

    .stat:first-child {
      padding-inline-start: 0;
      border-inline-start: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Value first in the DOM, which puts it first for a screen reader too: the
     strip is read "9/9 — slices independently reviewed", the same order a
     sighted visitor reads it. That makes the number the <dt>, inverting the
     usual term/description reading of a <dl>. The alternative — label as <dt>,
     value as <dd>, reordered visually with CSS — would buy a tidier semantic
     at the cost of a DOM/visual mismatch, which is the worse trade. The
     wrapper <div> around each pair is what HTML5 sanctions for grouping, and
     it is what gives the grid its four children. */
  /* Mono, because these are ledger readings and the mono face is this site's
     idiom for "this came out of the machine" — the same face as the transcript
     directly above. tabular-nums keeps the digits on a common width so the
     four figures align down the row.

     Aegean teal, not owl-gold: tokens.css assigns gold to links, the caret and
     key highlights (the hero already spends it on the caret and the primary
     CTA, and four gold numbers would stop it being sparing), and assigns teal
     to success states, which is what a measured proof number is. Secondary on
     background is 4.68:1 dark / 5.87:1 light — AA as normal text, and these
     render well above the 24px large-text threshold besides. */
  .stat dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: clamp(var(--text-xl), 4vw, var(--text-3xl));
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    color: var(--color-secondary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* The estimate is muted rather than teal, so the measured/estimated split
     ADR-0008 cares about is visible before a word is read — three teal
     readings and one that visibly is not one. This reinforces the copy's
     "est." prefix; it does not replace it, and neither may be dropped.
     Muted on background: 8.39:1 dark / 5.66:1 light, AA. */
  .stat--est dt {
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .stat dd {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  .proof-caption {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* --- Install one-liner ------------------------------------------------ */
  .install {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* --- Calls to action -------------------------------------------------- */
  .ctas {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
    max-width: none;
  }

  .cta {
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition:
      color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
      background-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out),
      border-color var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-out);
  }

  /* Accent as a background introduces one new pairing. Contrast is symmetric,
     so the tokens.css table already covers it: accent #D4A24E / bg #0E1116 =
     8.17:1 dark, accent #8A5A12 / bg #FAF7F0 = 5.53:1 light. Both AA. */
  .cta--primary {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    background-color: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-bg);
  }

  /* Hover inverts to the text/background pairing (17.68:1 in both schemes). */
  .cta--primary:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-text);
    background-color: var(--color-text);
    color: var(--color-bg);
  }

  /* The border is this control's only boundary cue, so it carries information
     and needs 3:1 (WCAG 1.4.11) — --color-border is a 1.40:1 hairline meant
     for decorative rules. --color-muted is 8.39:1 dark / 5.66:1 light. */
  .cta--secondary {
    border-color: var(--color-muted);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  .cta--secondary:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  /* --- Typing animation -------------------------------------------------
     Opt-in only: the rules above render the finished transcript, so users
     with `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` (and anyone whose CSS animations
     never run) see the complete final state with no delay. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .cmd {
      /* "$ metis next -o json" is exactly 20 characters, so 20ch is the
         finished width and steps(20) lands one character per step.
         content-box keeps the caret border outside that width — under the
         global border-box it would eat the last character. */
      box-sizing: content-box;
      width: 0;
      overflow: hidden;
      border-right: 0.6ch solid var(--color-accent);
      animation:
        type 650ms steps(20, end) 350ms forwards,
        caret-out 200ms var(--ease-out) 1000ms forwards;
    }

    .out {
      opacity: 0;
      animation: emit 220ms var(--ease-out) forwards;
    }

    /* Output starts once the command has been "entered" (1000ms) and prints
       a line every 145ms — the last line lands at ~2.6s. Children 2-11 are
       the ten output lines; child 1 is the command. */
    .line:nth-child(2) {
      animation-delay: 1100ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(3) {
      animation-delay: 1245ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(4) {
      animation-delay: 1390ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(5) {
      animation-delay: 1535ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(6) {
      animation-delay: 1680ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(7) {
      animation-delay: 1825ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(8) {
      animation-delay: 1970ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(9) {
      animation-delay: 2115ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(10) {
      animation-delay: 2260ms;
    }
    .line:nth-child(11) {
      animation-delay: 2405ms;
    }
  }

  @keyframes type {
    to {
      width: 20ch;
    }
  }

  /* The caret hands off to the "█" on the closing prompt line. */
  @keyframes caret-out {
    to {
      border-right-color: transparent;
    }
  }

  @keyframes emit {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(var(--space-1));
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
